### What is the difference between List and Set?

List and Set are both sub-interfaces of Collection, but they serve fundamentally different purposes.

A `List` is an ordered sequence of elements. It maintains insertion order, allows duplicate elements, and provides index-based access. You can ask "what is at position 5?" and get an answer in O(1) time with ArrayList.

A `Set` is a collection of unique elements. It does not allow duplicates – if you try to add an element that already exists (determined by `equals()`), the `add()` method returns `false` and the set remains unchanged. Sets do not provide index-based access.

| Feature | List | Set |
|---|---|---|
| Duplicates | Allowed | Not allowed |
| Ordering | Insertion order (always) | Depends on implementation |
| Index access | Yes – get(i), set(i, e) | No |
| Primary operation | Random access, iteration | Membership testing (contains) |
| contains() performance | O(n) for ArrayList | O(1) for HashSet |
| Implementations | ArrayList, LinkedList | HashSet, LinkedHashSet, TreeSet |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class ListVsSetDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // List – transaction log (duplicates OK, order matters)
        List<BigDecimal> transactionLog = new ArrayList<>();
        transactionLog.add(new BigDecimal("50000.00"));
        transactionLog.add(new BigDecimal("25000.00"));
        transactionLog.add(new BigDecimal("50000.00")); // duplicate allowed
        System.out.println("Transaction log: " + transactionLog);
        System.out.println("Third entry: " + transactionLog.get(2)); // index access

        // Set – unique account registry (no duplicates)
        Set<String> activeAccounts = new HashSet<>();
        activeAccounts.add("SAV2001");
        activeAccounts.add("CUR3001");
        boolean added = activeAccounts.add("SAV2001"); // returns false
        System.out.println("Duplicate added: " + added);
        System.out.println("Active accounts: " + activeAccounts);
    }
}
```

```text
Transaction log: [50000.00, 25000.00, 50000.00]
Third entry: 50000.00
Duplicate added: false
Active accounts: [CUR3001, SAV2001]
```

### Why does contains() performance matter?

The biggest practical difference between List and Set is the performance of the `contains()` method. In a banking system that processes millions of transactions, this difference decides whether a lookup takes microseconds or seconds.

`ArrayList.contains()` performs a linear scan – it checks every element one by one until it finds a match or reaches the end. This is O(n). For a list of 1 million accounts, it checks up to 1 million elements.

`HashSet.contains()` computes the hash code, jumps directly to the right bucket, and checks one or a few elements. This is O(1) on average. For 1 million accounts, it checks 1-2 elements.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class ContainsPerformanceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int size = 1_000_000;
        List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
        Set<String> set = new HashSet<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            String acc = "ACC" + String.format("%07d", i);
            list.add(acc);
            set.add(acc);
        }

        String target = "ACC0999999"; // last element

        // ArrayList.contains – O(n) scan
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        boolean found1 = list.contains(target);
        long listTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        // HashSet.contains – O(1) hash lookup
        start = System.nanoTime();
        boolean found2 = set.contains(target);
        long setTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.println("ArrayList.contains: " + listTime + " ns");
        System.out.println("HashSet.contains:   " + setTime + " ns");
        System.out.println("Set is ~" + (listTime / Math.max(setTime, 1)) + "x faster");
    }
}
```

```text
ArrayList.contains: 8542310 ns
HashSet.contains:   142 ns
Set is ~60156x faster
```

### When should you use a List?

Use a List when any of these conditions are true:

- **Duplicates are valid.** A transaction log contains the same amount multiple times, and each occurrence is a different event.
- **Order matters.** Statements must show transactions in chronological order.
- **Index access is needed.** You need to jump to a specific position: "show me the 50th transaction."
- **Iteration order must be predictable.** You process elements in a specific sequence.

Banking examples:

- Transaction history for an account (ordered, duplicates valid)
- List of EMI payment amounts (same amount repeats monthly)
- Page of search results (ordered by relevance, indexed for pagination)
- Batch of pending transfers to process in sequence

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.*;

public class TransactionService {
    record Transaction(String id, String accountId, BigDecimal amount, LocalDate date) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Transaction log – List because:
        // 1. Same amount can appear multiple times (valid business data)
        // 2. Chronological order is critical for statements
        // 3. We paginate using index: get(50) through get(59)
        List<Transaction> log = new ArrayList<>();
        log.add(new Transaction("TXN001", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000"), LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 15)));
        log.add(new Transaction("TXN002", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("25000"), LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 16)));
        log.add(new Transaction("TXN003", "SAV2001", new BigDecimal("50000"), LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 17))); // same amount

        // Pagination: show page 1 (items 0-1)
        int pageSize = 2;
        int page = 0;
        List<Transaction> pageItems = log.subList(page * pageSize, Math.min((page + 1) * pageSize, log.size()));
        pageItems.forEach(t -> System.out.println(t.id() + ": " + t.amount()));
    }
}
```

```text
TXN001: 50000
TXN002: 25000
```

### When should you use a Set?

Use a Set when any of these conditions are true:

- **Uniqueness is a business requirement.** Each account number must appear exactly once.
- **The primary operation is membership testing.** "Does this account exist?" is called thousands of times per second.
- **You need to eliminate duplicates.** Converting a List to a Set removes duplicates automatically.
- **You need mathematical set operations.** Intersection, union, and difference are natural with Sets.

Banking examples:

- Active account number registry (uniqueness enforced)
- Set of blocked cards (fast lookup: "is this card blocked?")
- Set of countries for sanctions screening (fast membership check)
- Unique IP addresses that accessed an account (fraud detection)

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class FraudDetectionService {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Blocked accounts – Set because uniqueness is mandatory
        Set<String> blockedAccounts = new HashSet<>();
        blockedAccounts.add("SAV9999");
        blockedAccounts.add("CUR8888");

        // Fast membership check before processing transfer
        String sourceAccount = "SAV2001";
        if (blockedAccounts.contains(sourceAccount)) {
            System.out.println("BLOCKED: " + sourceAccount);
        } else {
            System.out.println("OK: " + sourceAccount + " can proceed");
        }

        // Eliminate duplicates from transaction IPs
        List<String> loginIPs = List.of("10.0.0.1", "10.0.0.2", "10.0.0.1", "10.0.0.3", "10.0.0.1");
        Set<String> uniqueIPs = new HashSet<>(loginIPs);
        System.out.println("Unique IPs: " + uniqueIPs);
        System.out.println("Login attempts: " + loginIPs.size() + ", unique: " + uniqueIPs.size());

        // Set operations – intersection (common IPs with another account)
        Set<String> otherAccountIPs = Set.of("10.0.0.1", "10.0.0.5");
        Set<String> commonIPs = new HashSet<>(uniqueIPs);
        commonIPs.retainAll(otherAccountIPs); // intersection
        System.out.println("Common IPs (fraud signal): " + commonIPs);
    }
}
```

```text
OK: SAV2001 can proceed
Unique IPs: [10.0.0.3, 10.0.0.1, 10.0.0.2]
Login attempts: 5, unique: 3
Common IPs (fraud signal): [10.0.0.1]
```

### What if you need both uniqueness and ordering?

This is a common follow-up question. Java provides two Set implementations that maintain order:

- **LinkedHashSet** – maintains insertion order. Elements iterate in the order they were added. Useful when you need uniqueness but also need deterministic, predictable output (for audits, reports, tests).
- **TreeSet** – maintains sorted order. Elements iterate in natural order (or a custom Comparator). Useful for sorted unique data like account numbers in alphabetical order.

| Need | Use |
|---|---|
| Uniqueness only, no order needed | HashSet |
| Uniqueness + insertion order | LinkedHashSet |
| Uniqueness + sorted order | TreeSet |
| Ordered + duplicates + index access | ArrayList |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class OrderedSetDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // HashSet – no order guarantee
        Set<String> hashSet = new HashSet<>();
        hashSet.add("CUR3001");
        hashSet.add("SAV2001");
        hashSet.add("FD4001");
        System.out.println("HashSet:       " + hashSet);

        // LinkedHashSet – insertion order preserved
        Set<String> linkedSet = new LinkedHashSet<>();
        linkedSet.add("CUR3001");
        linkedSet.add("SAV2001");
        linkedSet.add("FD4001");
        System.out.println("LinkedHashSet: " + linkedSet);

        // TreeSet – sorted order
        Set<String> treeSet = new TreeSet<>();
        treeSet.add("CUR3001");
        treeSet.add("SAV2001");
        treeSet.add("FD4001");
        System.out.println("TreeSet:       " + treeSet);
    }
}
```

```text
HashSet:       [FD4001, CUR3001, SAV2001]
LinkedHashSet: [CUR3001, SAV2001, FD4001]
TreeSet:       [CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2001]
```

### What are common mistakes when choosing between List and Set?

#### Mistake 1: Using ArrayList for membership testing

```java
// WRONG – O(n) contains on every transfer
List<String> blockedAccounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of("SAV9999", "CUR8888"));
if (blockedAccounts.contains(sourceAccount)) { } // scans entire list

// CORRECT – O(1) contains
Set<String> blockedAccountsSet = new HashSet<>(List.of("SAV9999", "CUR8888"));
if (blockedAccountsSet.contains(sourceAccount)) { } // hash lookup
```

#### Mistake 2: Using Set when you need index access

```java
// WRONG – Set has no get(index)
Set<String> accounts = new HashSet<>();
// accounts.get(0); // compile error – Set has no get() method

// CORRECT – use List for index access
List<String> accountList = new ArrayList<>(accounts);
String first = accountList.get(0);
```

#### Mistake 3: Forgetting that Set uses equals/hashCode

```java
// If your class does not override equals/hashCode,
// two objects with the same data are treated as different
class Account {
    String id;
    Account(String id) { this.id = id; }
    // No equals/hashCode override!
}

Set<Account> accounts = new HashSet<>();
accounts.add(new Account("SAV2001"));
accounts.add(new Account("SAV2001")); // treated as different!
System.out.println(accounts.size()); // 2 – not 1!
```

### What about thread safety?

Neither ArrayList nor HashSet is thread-safe. For concurrent access:

- List: use `CopyOnWriteArrayList` (read-heavy) or `Collections.synchronizedList()` (write-heavy).
- Set: use `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` (Java 8+) or `Collections.synchronizedSet()`.

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

// Thread-safe Set using ConcurrentHashMap
Set<String> concurrentSet = ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet();
concurrentSet.add("SAV2001");
concurrentSet.add("CUR3001");
// Safe for concurrent add/remove/contains without external sync
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Memory footprint.** A `HashSet<String>` with 1000 elements uses roughly 48 KB (because it wraps a `HashMap` internally – each element becomes a key with a dummy value). An `ArrayList<String>` with 1000 elements uses roughly 4 KB (just the reference array). If memory is tight and you only need iteration, not fast contains, ArrayList is cheaper.

- **Set.of() iteration order.** `Set.of()` (Java 9+) does not guarantee any specific iteration order, and the order can change between JVM runs. Do not rely on it for deterministic output.

- **EnumSet.** When your set contains enum constants, always use `EnumSet` instead of `HashSet`. It uses a bit vector internally and is faster and uses less memory than any other Set implementation.

- **Converting between List and Set.** `new ArrayList<>(set)` converts a Set to a List. `new HashSet<>(list)` converts a List to a Set (removing duplicates). `new LinkedHashSet<>(list)` preserves order while removing duplicates.

- **Set operations.** `retainAll()` is intersection. `addAll()` is union. `removeAll()` is difference. These are defined on `Collection` but most useful with Sets. They modify the calling set in place.
